   "text": "The drawing shows a steroid skeleton (four fused rings, 17 ring carbons), and I matched it to boldenone by reading it region by region:\n\n- **Ring A:** a cross-conjugated dienone, with C1=C2, a C3 ketone (C=O) and C4=C5.\n- **C10:** a hashed methyl group, which is the 19-methyl.\n- **C13:** a hashed methyl group, which is the 18-methyl.\n- **C17:** a hashed OH on the five-membered ring D.\n- **Ring-fusion hydrogens:** a wedge H at C9, a hashed H at C8 and a wedge H at C14.\n\nThe molecule is drawn mirrored left to right (ring A on the right, ring D on the left), which swaps wedges and hashes. Once that is accounted for, every stereocentre matches the natural configuration: 8\u03b2-H, 9\u03b1-H, 14\u03b1-H, 10\u03b2-methyl, 13\u03b2-methyl and 17\u03b2-OH. This is 17\u03b2-hydroxyandrosta-1,4-dien-3-one.\n\nI did not derive the @/@@ marks atom by atom from the drawing. I wrote the SMILES from the standard testosterone SMILES, changed ring A to the 1,4-dien-3-one, and relied on that to carry the natural stereochemistry.\n\nSMILES: C[C@]12CC[C@H]3[C@H]([C@@H]1CC[C@@H]2O)CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]34C"
